# libxtc Valgrind suppressions -- known-benign runtime internals.
#
# These are NOT bugs.  Valgrind's race detectors (Helgrind, DRD) cannot
# see through the runtime's atomic lifecycle handshakes, so a small set
# of teardown patterns are reported as data races.  Each entry below is
# a specific, understood false positive -- not a blanket silence.
#
# memcheck runs with an EMPTY-effect against this file (no memcheck
# entries here): the leak/error gate is meant to stay honest, so if
# memcheck ever fires we want it red.  Helgrind/DRD are informational
# (they do not gate the job) but still load this file so their report
# is signal rather than noise.
#
# To regenerate/extend: run under a tool with --gen-suppressions=all
# and paste the emitted stanza here with a comment explaining WHY it is
# benign.

# --- Helgrind ---------------------------------------------------------

# lock_mgr's deadlock-detector thread reads a lockmgr field while the
# owning thread tears the manager down.  The detector is joined/stopped
# as part of destroy; the read is on a copy that is race-free in program
# order, but Helgrind cannot see the stop handshake.  Benign teardown.
{
   xtc_lockmgr_detector_teardown_helgrind
   Helgrind:Race
   fun:__detector_thread
}
{
   xtc_lockmgr_destroy_teardown_helgrind
   Helgrind:Race
   fun:xtc_lockmgr_destroy
   fun:test_deadlock
}

# --- DRD --------------------------------------------------------------

# Same lock_mgr detector-thread teardown pattern as above, seen by DRD.
{
   xtc_lockmgr_detector_teardown_drd
   drd:ConflictingAccess
   fun:__detector_thread
}
{
   xtc_lockmgr_destroy_teardown_drd
   drd:ConflictingAccess
   fun:xtc_lockmgr_destroy
   fun:test_deadlock
}
